;; Docs for std/core-macros.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "core-macros.bjodoc").
;; The texts are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "The macros below the prelude: cond. The prelude re-exports them, so a program does not import this module.")
  (see cond))

(:doc cond
  (summary "Picks the first clause whose test holds, at any number of arms; the last clause is else.")
  (form (cond clause ... (else body ...)))
  (rest clause """
    A clause, one of:

    @code{(test body ...)}: evaluates the body when @code{test} is true.

    @code{(:match pattern expr body ...)}, or
    @code{(:match pattern expr #:when guard body ...)}: evaluates the body when
    @code{expr} matches @code{pattern} and the guard, if any, holds.

    @code{(:def pattern expr)}, or @code{(:def pattern expr failure ...)}:
    binds locally; does not produce a value.

    @code{(:do body ...)}: runs the body for side effects; does not produce a value.
    """)
  (rest body "The forms evaluated in the else clause; the final form is returned.")
  (literal else)
  (example """
    (cond ((< n 0) "negative")
          ((= n 0) "zero")
          (else "positive"))

    ;; A value a later test needs, without a nested cond to hold it
    (cond ((string-empty? text) "empty")
          (:def first (string-cursor-ref text (string-cursor-start text)))
          ((char-numeric? first) "number")
          (else "word"))
    """)
  (reference """
    @code{if} at any number of arms. The final @code{else} is required, since
    every arm must evaluate to a value and the branches must be exhaustive;
    every arm needs the same type. Only the clause that is taken runs. A clause
    with a test and no body is an error, and so is a clause after the else, which
    could never be reached.

    A @code{:match} clause is a test that binds: what its pattern binds is in
    scope in its guard and body only, and when it does not match the cond
    goes on to the next clause.

    @code{:def} and @code{:do} do not produce values. Both are reached only when
    every test above them has failed. A @code{:def} binds, and what it binds
    is in scope in every clause below it and nowhere else. It @strong{is} a
    @code{def} whose body is the rest of the cond, so it takes the same
    patterns and the same failure parts: @code{(:def (Ok v) (parse s) :propagate)}
    makes the whole cond the @code{Err}, and
    @code{(:def (Some n) (lookup k) :default 0)} binds @code{n} either way and
    never terminates the cond early. A @code{:do} runs its forms and goes on.
    A cond may not end in either.

    The @code{else} clause unconditionally evaluates its body without looking
    at anything after it, so clauses written after it are not used.
    """))
